WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.251
Primitive obsession appears when
meaningful domain concepts remain represented as

2
00:00:04.331 --> 00:00:06.207
raw strings, integers, booleans, tuples,

3
00:00:06.287 --> 00:00:08.891
or loosely structured maps long after
parsing is complete.

4
00:00:08.971 --> 00:00:12.254
A `String` might mean a username, tenant
identifier, path fragment, role,

5
00:00:12.334 --> 00:00:14.001
status, token label, or display name.

6
00:00:14.081 --> 00:00:17.623
A `u32` might mean a count, port, timeout,
user ID, protocol field, or size limit.

7
00:00:17.703 --> 00:00:20.100
When too many concepts share the same
primitive shape,

8
00:00:20.180 --> 00:00:21.743
accidental mixing becomes easier.

9
00:00:21.823 --> 00:00:24.219
The compiler can tell that two values are
both strings,

10
00:00:24.299 --> 00:00:27.738
but it cannot know that one is a tenant ID
and the other is a path component unless

11
00:00:27.818 --> 00:00:29.225
the code models those concepts.

12
00:00:29.305 --> 00:00:32.326
Validation also tends to become scattered,
repeated, or forgotten.

13
00:00:32.406 --> 00:00:34.751
Weak type design leaves invalid states
expressible.

14
00:00:34.831 --> 00:00:37.175
A boolean pair might allow impossible
combinations.

15
00:00:37.255 --> 00:00:39.547
A stringly typed status might accept
misspellings.

16
00:00:39.627 --> 00:00:42.753
A raw integer limit might be negative in
one layer, too large in another,

17
00:00:42.833 --> 00:00:44.136
or measured in the wrong unit.

18
00:00:44.216 --> 00:00:45.935
These problems are not Rust-specific,

19
00:00:46.015 --> 00:00:48.307
but Rust gives teams excellent tools to
reduce them.

20
00:00:48.387 --> 00:00:51.774
Newtypes make domain meaning visible while
keeping representation simple.

21
00:00:51.854 --> 00:00:54.250
Enums model states, roles, modes, and
finite choices.

22
00:00:54.330 --> 00:00:57.612
Validated constructors can ensure that a
value meets range, format, unit,

23
00:00:57.692 --> 00:01:00.558
and policy requirements before it enters
the core of the program.

24
00:01:00.638 --> 00:01:03.972
`Option` and `Result` can show absence and
validation failure explicitly.

25
00:01:04.052 --> 00:01:07.386
A strong review question is whether a
primitive still belongs at that layer.

26
00:01:07.466 --> 00:01:09.029
Raw input is normal at the boundary.

27
00:01:09.109 --> 00:01:11.818
Deeper in the system, important values
should carry meaning.

28
00:01:11.898 --> 00:01:13.826
Good type design reduces scattered checks,

29
00:01:13.906 --> 00:01:15.625
makes invalid states harder to create,

30
00:01:15.705 --> 00:01:19.300
and gives security reviewers a clearer map
of what the program believes to be true.
